    "reading": {
        "body": "Virginia holds 330 of the 1,843 data center facilities counted here, more than Oregon and Texas combined and roughly one in six of the total.\n\nThe distribution is steep. The ten largest states account for 1,023 sites, about 56 percent, and the other 37 jurisdictions share the remainder, many with fewer than ten each. Oregon and Texas tie at 133, California has 103, Ohio 77, Arizona 57, Iowa 52, New Jersey 48, Washington 46, Illinois 44. Outside the United States the list is much narrower, eight countries only, led by Germany at 197, then China at 103, Italy at 52, Spain at 39, and India at 2.\n\nFor AI, this measures buildings rather than compute. A facility count says how many sites exist in a state, not how much power they draw or how many accelerators they hold. One hyperscale campus can consume more electricity than a dozen small colocation sites, and nothing here separates the two. The concentration in Virginia, Texas, and Oregon is consistent with places that had cheap power and available land before the current buildout, but the data cannot confirm that, because it carries no megawatts, no prices, and no capacity figures.\n\nThree limits are worth stating. The state entries sum to 1,394, which is 449 short of the stated 1,843, while the eight international entries sum to 448, so the headline figure appears to be global rather than US-only. The list contains both \"OH\" at 77 and \"Ohio\" at 5, so the totals need cleaning before they are treated as exact. And this is a single snapshot with no operator names and no history, so it cannot show which states are gaining sites, which companies are building them, or whether any of it is tied to AI workloads specifically.",
